# =============================================================================
# restic backup -- per-host configuration  (SAMPLE / template)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy to ~/.config/restic/config and fill in the values for THIS host:
#     cp config.sample ~/.config/restic/config
#     chmod 600 ~/.config/restic/config
#
# SECURITY -- the real config holds the REST password, the ntfy token, and the
# healthchecks ping URL. DO NOT commit the filled-in file. Commit only this
# .sample (placeholders). Add to .gitignore:   config   encryption-pw
# (Or keep both out of the repo entirely / encrypt with sops or git-crypt.)
#
# This file is sourced by the backup script as plain bash: normal quoting rules
# apply, and arrays use (parentheses).
# =============================================================================

# ---- PATH (only if restic is somewhere unusual) -----------------------------
# The script runs with a fixed PATH of /usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:
# /usr/bin:/sbin:/bin and does NOT inherit the caller's, so that a poisoned
# environment cannot decide which `restic` or `ip` it runs. If restic lives
# outside those -- a snap (/snap/bin), /opt, a Go workspace -- add it here. This
# file is only sourced after its ownership has been verified.
# export PATH="/snap/bin:$PATH"

# ---- Repository (where backups go) -----------------------------------------
# rest-server URL. The host is reachable only over the WireGuard tunnel.
export RESTIC_REPOSITORY="rest:http://10.0.0.2:8000/CHANGE-ME/"

# rest-server HTTP Basic-Auth credentials (NOT the encryption password).
# IMPORTANT: with rest-server --private-repos, RESTIC_REST_USERNAME MUST equal
# the first path segment of RESTIC_REPOSITORY, or every request gets 401.
# e.g. repo ".../-laptop/"  =>  username "laptop".
export RESTIC_REST_USERNAME="CHANGE-ME"
export RESTIC_REST_PASSWORD="CHANGE-ME"            # SECRET -- do not commit

# Repository ENCRYPTION password, read from a 0600 file (separate secret, also
# never committed). This is what actually protects the data at rest.
export RESTIC_PASSWORD_FILE="$HOME/.config/restic/encryption-pw"

# ---- What to back up --------------------------------------------------------
# Required, must be non-empty. Whole-system backup = (/).
BACKUP_PATHS=(/)

# Extra args for `restic backup`. --one-file-system keeps restic from wandering
# into other mounts (USB disks, network shares) that the exclude file might not
# cover -- but it is a blunt instrument, and it stops just as quietly at /home,
# /var or /srv when those are separate logical volumes, which is the normal LVM
# and cloud-image layout. A snapshot then holds the root filesystem and nothing
# else, and nothing says so until a restore.
#
# So the script names every mounted filesystem inside BACKUP_PATHS that this
# flag is skipping, and pages about it -- while still backing up everything it
# can reach, because a snapshot missing one mount beats no snapshot at all. The
# answer is one of: add it to BACKUP_PATHS, exclude it, or declare it below.
EXTRA_BACKUP_ARGS=(--one-file-system)

# Mounts that are deliberately NOT backed up -- scratch space, a media library
# with its own copy, a share that belongs to another host. Listing one here is
# what turns "this would be silently skipped" into a decision you made.
UNBACKED_MOUNTS=()    # e.g. (/mnt/scratch /srv/media)

# Exclude file (defaults to $CONFIG_DIR/excludes if unset). `excludes` has no
# secrets, IS committed to the repo, and is OVERWRITTEN on every deploy -- it is
# the fleet-wide base. Do not hand-edit it on a host.
# EXCLUDE_FILE="$HOME/.config/restic/excludes"

# This host's own patterns (defaults to $CONFIG_DIR/excludes.local if unset).
# Hand-managed, never deployed, and OPTIONAL -- most hosts have none. It is read
# AFTER the file above, so it can add patterns and also take one back with a
# leading "!". That is where a per-host decision belongs: see
# excludes.local.sample. Both files are passed to restic, in that order.
# EXCLUDE_FILE_LOCAL="$HOME/.config/restic/excludes.local"

# ---- Database dumps (declarative) -------------------------------------------
# Declare databases here and the main script dumps them natively, before the
# backup, into $DUMP_DIR -- which is added to the backup set and wiped on exit,
# so the plaintext never lingers. ANY dump failure aborts the whole run.
#
# A host with no databases just leaves all four arrays empty.
#
#   ALL      -> ONE combined dump (mariadb-dump --all-databases / pg_dumpall).
#   db names -> one file per database (dedups better; restore individually).
#
# Credentials: local MariaDB via ~/.my.cnf, local PostgreSQL via `sudo -n -u
# postgres` (peer auth), and DOCKER_AUTO from each container's OWN environment
# (MARIADB_ROOT_PASSWORD / MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD / POSTGRES_PASSWORD) -- so a
# password is never visible in the host process list.

MARIADB_LOCAL=()      # (ALL) or (db1 db2)              -- local MariaDB/MySQL
POSTGRES_LOCAL=()     # (ALL) or (db1 db2)              -- local PostgreSQL
DOCKER_AUTO=()        # (app-mariadb analytics-pg ...)  -- name the CONTAINER only:
                      #   the engine is detected inside it and EVERYTHING is dumped
SQLITE_FILES=()       # (name:/path/to.db ...)          -- host path, incl. bind mounts

# ---- Pre-backup hook (optional escape hatch) --------------------------------
# For anything the arrays above cannot express: a container-only SQLite file, a
# DB password kept in a host secret file, quiescing a service, a non-DB export.
# See ./pre-backup. Skipped unless the file exists AND is executable, so hosts
# with nothing to prepare simply omit it. Runs AFTER the native dumps.
# PRE_BACKUP_HOOK="$HOME/.config/restic/pre-backup"
# DUMP_DIR="$HOME/.config/restic/db-dumps"

# ---- Scheduling (the script gates itself; cron just calls it hourly) --------
# Cron runs this every hour and the script decides whether this hour is the
# moment. Reading order: run inside BACKUP_WINDOW, but not if a run already
# succeeded within MIN_INTERVAL_HOURS, and ignore the window entirely once the
# last success is FORCE_AFTER_HOURS old.
#
# Local hours, "start-end", end exclusive; wraps midnight. "" = no window.
BACKUP_WINDOW="23-06"

# Don't repeat a successful backup sooner than this. Keep it BELOW 24 -- at 24
# a run that lands at 01:00 makes the next one eligible at 01:00, and that
# ratchet walks the backup later every day until it falls out of the window.
MIN_INTERVAL_HOURS="20"

# Catch-up. Plain cron does NOT replay jobs missed while the machine was off or
# asleep, so on a laptop this is the ONLY thing that ever triggers a backup --
# the 23-06 window is never satisfied by a machine that sleeps at night. It then
# runs at whatever daytime hour the laptop happens to be awake. Don't set it to 0.
FORCE_AFTER_HOURS="24"

# ---- Staleness = the hard fail ----------------------------------------------
# Skipping is always silent; what turns silence into an alert is age. Every
# path that declines to back up (not due, metered, tunnel down) checks this
# first, so a host that quietly stops backing up pages you locally instead of
# waiting on the external
# dead-man's switch. Losing the lock to a still-running backup is checked
# against how long THAT run has been going instead -- the last success says
# nothing about a run that has not finished -- but against this same limit.
#
#   servers          : 36   (one missed night is fine; two is not)
#   laptop that travels: 168 (a week away from the tunnel is normal)
#   0                : never hard-fail on age (you then have ONLY the DMS)
#
# Must be greater than FORCE_AFTER_HOURS, or you get paged about backups that
# were not due yet. The script warns at startup if it isn't.
MAX_BACKUP_AGE_HOURS="36"

# While stale, re-page at most this often. 24 invocations a day must not mean
# 24 pushes: the first failure after a success always pages, further failures
# are log-only until the MAX_BACKUP_AGE crossing, and from there it repeats at
# this interval until a backup succeeds.
#
#   0 = escalate once at the MAX_BACKUP_AGE crossing and then stay quiet until
#       a backup succeeds (you still have the log and the dead-man's switch).
NOTIFY_REPEAT_HOURS="12"

# ---- Network gating ---------------------------------------------------------
# Skip on metered links (cellular cost). TRUE on laptops, FALSE on wired servers.
SKIP_IF_METERED="true"

# Reachability probe. Any HTTP response (even 401) counts as "reachable" -- this
# only tests that the WG route is up, not auth. Point at the rest-server root.
REST_HEALTH_URL="http://10.0.0.2:8000/"

# ---- WireGuard self-heal (optional) -----------------------------------------
# If the probe above fails, bounce the tunnel and try again. This targets
# the tunnel that is "up" but dead: the peer's endpoint moved (dynamic DNS, new
# NAT mapping) and the kernel keeps talking to the old address forever.
#
# Name the interface to enable it; "" leaves the tunnel alone. Requires root.
# The script prefers `systemctl restart wg-quick@<if>` when that unit is active
# and only falls back to `wg-quick down/up` otherwise -- going behind systemd's
# back would leave the unit thinking the interface is still up. It also refuses
# to touch the tunnel when there is no default route: the host is simply offline,
# `wg-quick up` could not resolve the endpoint, and a failed up() after a
# successful down() leaves you worse off than before. That refusal covers
# WG_RESTART_CMD too -- a custom restart cannot reach a DNS server either.
WG_INTERFACE=""                                    # e.g. "wg0"

# Escape hatch for tunnels managed by something else (NetworkManager, a custom
# unit). If set, this replaces the systemctl / wg-quick restart above, and
# WG_INTERFACE becomes only a label. It does NOT bypass the two guards: the
# no-default-route refusal and WG_BOUNCE_INTERVAL_HOURS below are both checked
# before either path runs.
# WG_RESTART_CMD="nmcli connection up wg0"

# Seconds to wait after a restart before re-probing.
# WG_SETTLE_SECS="5"

# Don't bounce again within this many hours. Cron calls the script hourly and
# past FORCE_AFTER_HOURS every one of those runs is due, so without this a
# backend that is genuinely down for two days would be met with 48 tunnel
# restarts. The FIRST bounce of an outage is always immediate -- this only
# spaces the retries -- and a successful backup resets it.
#   0 = no limit (bounce on every due run)
# WG_BOUNCE_INTERVAL_HOURS="6"

# ---- Failure notifications (ntfy) -- failure-only; success is silent --------
# Your ntfy base URL (self-hosted or https://ntfy.sh). No trailing slash.
NTFY_URL="https://ntfy.example.com"
NTFY_TOPIC_HIGH="backups-high"                     # topic for urgent failures
NTFY_TOPIC_LOW=""                                  # topic for version drift; "" = log only
# SECRET -- do not commit. An empty token only works on a topic with no access
# control, and on ntfy.sh (and any ntfy with default ACLs) that means ANYONE who
# guesses the topic name can read it -- "backups-high" being an obvious guess.
# The messages carry the hostname, the failing stage, the time of the last good
# backup and 1500 bytes of restic / pg_dumpall stderr: host inventory, paths,
# database names, and a live signal of which host has stopped backing up. Use a
# token and an access-controlled topic; if you must run an open one, make the
# topic name a random string and treat it as the only thing keeping it private.
NTFY_TOKEN="CHANGE-ME"

# ---- Dead-man's-switch ping (healthchecks.io or self-hosted) ----------------
# Script pings $URL/start at begin, $URL on success, $URL/fail on failure. If
# the pings stop, the service alerts you. Leave empty ("") to disable.
RESTIC_PING_URL="https://hc-ping.com/CHANGE-ME"    # treat as a secret -- do not commit

# ---- Version drift (report only -- nothing here ever updates the script) ----
# Compares the installed restic-backup.sh against the published one and says so
# in the log, in --status, and (if NTFY_TOPIC_LOW is set) with ONE low-priority
# push per newly published version. It answers "which host is running an old
# copy?", which is the only part of self-updating worth having.
#
# It deliberately cannot install anything. The script runs as root on every
# host: a fetch-and-exec would turn one GitHub credential into fleet-wide root,
# and with an hourly schedule it would land within the hour. Updates are pushed
# by ./deploy.sh, from a machine you are sitting at.
#
# The check runs only AFTER a successful backup, never before, and every error
# in it is swallowed -- it can neither delay nor block a backup. "" disables it.
VERSION_CHECK_URL="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ImanuelBertrand/backup-scripts/refs/heads/main/restic-backup.sh"
VERSION_CHECK_INTERVAL_HOURS="24"

# ---- Misc -------------------------------------------------------------------
# How long `restic backup` waits for a held repo lock (e.g. during maintenance
# prune on the server) before giving up. Default 15m if unset.
# LOCK_WAIT="15m"
